{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["Two shoulder-pieces, etc. — The ephod, in my understanding, is made like a half-garment, from a person's waist downward, covering him both in front and in back. This is what is written: ויחגר אותו בחשב האפוד ויאפוד לו בו — the חשב is made like a kind of girdle fashioned within the edge of the ephod, in the body of the ephod. And these two shoulder-pieces are joined to each other completely, and cover the person's entire body — in back, from his waist upward. And this is why it writes a second time 'וחבר': besides the shoulder-pieces being joined each to its counterpart up to the person's shoulders above, he goes back and joins them from below to the ephod. And the breastpiece is in front, over his heart, a span its length and a span its width, joined above to the shoulder-pieces and below to the ephod — so the whole becomes one garment. And thus the entire body of the priest is covered by the ephod, which is joined to the breastpiece and to the shoulder-pieces of the ephod. For if the shoulder-pieces were not joined together but were merely two straps going up over his neck and attaching to the chains of the breastpiece in front — then when the priest is occupied in the service and bends his neck downward, the shoulder-pieces would fall and separate and the breastpiece would fall." "To its two ends — which are joined at the priest's back; the upper ends of the ephod, above the חשב."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:43:24.793283817Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}